        "evidence": "Table only.",
        "notes": "RECOMPUTED ALL 20 ROWS (25 dps). Internal arithmetic is CLEAN: Delta = pred - obs and Delta/m = 100 Delta/obs reproduce on every row to the tabulated precision; zero rows fail. The observed column is atomic (H at 938.783 = m_p + m_e - 13.6 eV, He4 at 3728.401), which is the right convention. THE CONSEQUENTIAL FINDING is the excision issue: these are ABSOLUTE atomic masses in MeV, which under the 2026-08-11 owner ruling are never a framework prediction, and the Delta/m column measures the error against a total that is ~99.9% free-nucleon rest mass supplied by CODATA. Recomputing the error against the quantity the framework actually supplies — the nuclear binding energy B = Z m_H + N m_n - M_atom — inverts the impression completely: He4 B = 28.296 MeV, err +22.0% (tabulated +0.167%); Be9 B = 58.165, err -27.7% (tabulated -0.192%); B11 -23.4%; N14 -13.2%; C12 -9.8%; Ar40 +14.3%; Ca40 +9.9%; O16 -6.6%; median |err/B| about 8%, range 0.4% to 28%. So the table's sub-0.2% column overstates the accuracy of the framework-supplied part by two to three orders of magnitude. The parent remark rem:mass-table-z1-20 already says the rows are not reproducible from a committed witness and that the live pipelines give much larger errors, but it does not make this point, and the table block carries no caveat at all."
      }
